WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.715
Some adversaries deliberately study industrial environments and design activity around

00:00:04.715 --> 00:00:05.775
process operations.

00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:10.730
Their objective may be espionage, strategic access, loss of control, reduced reliability,

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:11.700
or physical disruption.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.000
These threats are less common than routine malware and misconfiguration,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.490
but their potential consequence justifies focused preparation in high-impact environments.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:23.100
Process knowledge can increase an adversary's impact because the meaning of a command

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.200
depends on equipment state,

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.400
timing, safeguards, and operator response.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.705
Safety-related systems deserve particular protection and independent analysis.

00:00:30.705 --> 00:00:34.370
Defenders should avoid assuming that separation by name alone makes a safety function

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.040
unreachable or immune from shared dependencies.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.060
Advanced actors may also use legitimate administration and engineering capabilities

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.700
already present in the environment.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.000
This is often described as living off the land.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.900
The defensive implication is that detecting a known malware file is not enough.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.315
Teams need visibility into who used sensitive tools, from which workstation,

00:00:52.315 --> 00:00:54.640
against which asset, under what change record,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.365
and whether the activity matched expected operations.

00:00:57.365 --> 00:01:01.200
High-level threat modeling can identify critical functions, engineering pathways,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.100
trust boundaries,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.150
and conditions that could lead to unsafe or unreliable operation without documenting

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.370
harmful procedures.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:10.300
Monitoring should prioritize unexpected configuration activity,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.730
unusual cross-level communications, changes to trusted baselines,

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.610
and access outside approved windows.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.120
Resilience requires independent safeguards, segmented architecture,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.170
protected engineering systems, recoverable configurations, coordinated response,

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.295
and operators trained to recognize loss of trusted control or visibility.

00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:31.170
Advanced-threat preparation should strengthen defenses against ordinary incidents as well.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.220
Clear ownership, strong access control,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:37.430
and evidence-based change management reduce the opportunity for both sophisticated actors

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.580
and routine mistakes.
